/E (Traditionally, employee performance was mainly evaluated by the immediate supervisor or manager. Although this method can provide useful information, a single person may not observe every aspect of an employee's behaviour and performance. Modern organizations therefore use different approaches to obtain a broader understanding of employee performance.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
107 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (One such approach is 360-degree feedback. It is also called multi-source feedback or multi-rater feedback. In this method, feedback is collected from different people who interact with the employee. These may include supervisors, colleagues, subordinates, customers and the employee themselves.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
108 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (The purpose of 360-degree feedback is to provide an employee with a more comprehensive understanding of their strengths and areas that require improvement. Instead of receiving feedback from only one manager, the employee receives information from several perspectives. 360-degree feedback can be particularly useful for evaluating behaviours such as communication, teamwork, leadership, problem-solving, interpersonal skills and professionalism. It can also help employees understand how their behaviour is perceived by others.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
